The review is complete at `/Users/rich/source/HARES/docs/reviews/hpxml/hpxml-02-wh-jacket-ua-reduction-mismatch.md`.

**Key conclusion**: HARES does apply jacket UA reduction at equipment initialization (before the energy balance), so the feared "silently overestimate standby losses" scenario does not occur. However, HARES uses a different physics model (series-resistance: `1/new_ua = 1/ua_base + jacket_r/A`) vs OCHRE's skin-conduction model (computing reduction from wall U-value and subtracting from total UA). This produces materially different numerical results — HARES predicts ~3.5x higher UA for a typical jacketed electric WH compared to OCHRE. One medium-severity finding and one low-severity finding documented.
